// ProgressRing: A ring that shows that something is in progress (WinUI's `ProgressRing`): a
// spinning arc while [`is_indeterminate`](Self::is_indeterminate) (the default), the part of a
// ring of the [`value`](Self::value) between [`minimum`](Self::minimum) and
// [`maximum`](Self::maximum) otherwise, and nothing while not [`is_active`](Self::is_active).
// As in WinUI, `Value`, `Minimum` and `Maximum` are properties of the ring (not of
// `RangeBase`), coerced as `ProgressRing.cpp` coerces them: the bound set last wins, and a
// value outside the range is moved into it. The control sets the states of WinUI as
// pseudo-classes, which the control theme of the WinUI theme
// (`src/FerroUI.Themes.WinUI/Controls/ProgressRing.xaml`) styles: `:active` (Active),
// `:determinate-active` (DeterminateActive) and `:inactive` (Inactive). WinUI draws the ring
// with an `AnimatedVisualPlayer` and the Lottie compositions of its animated visuals, which
// the code plays. FerroUI has no player: the template draws the ring with `Arc` shapes, and
// the control moves the arc of the template part `PART_Indicator` as the code of WinUI plays
// the compositions: the indeterminate composition loops over two seconds (the arc grows to
// half the ring and shrinks while it turns 900 degrees), and a determinate change plays the
// determinate composition from the old to the new value (two seconds for the whole ring), a
// decrease at once. The clock is the ring's `Clock`, or the global clock; the motion stops
// while the ring is inactive, hidden or out of the visual tree. `DeterminateSource` and
// `IndeterminateSource` (preview members of WinUI) are not ported. The Fluent and Simple
// themes have no template for it (docs/porting/winui-controls.md, section 5.1).
type ProgressRing struct{ TemplatedControl }

